{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792850758,"nonce":"hTJo+Tplf0qTKcVUighMxo3O8r0=","title":"TypeScript: implicit derived constructors, redeclared fields and polymorphic this","message":"Three inheritance gaps from 1165e1b. A derived class without a constructor takes its base constructor's parameters (with their optional and rest forms) and forwards them to the base initializer. A field that redeclares an inherited one with a value keeps the inherited layout slot and runs its initializer after super(...), in source order with the class's own fields; an unannotated redeclaration takes the inherited type, a different type is TypeScript's 2416, and narrowing visibility is 2415. Redeclaring without a value stays Unsupported, since its effect depends on the class-field semantics the target selects. A method whose result is inferred from `return this` on every path is typed by the receiver's class when called through a subclass, as TypeScript's polymorphic this; other inferred class results through subclasses stay Unsupported.","files":null}]}